  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1211 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nUpper level high pressure will move very little through the mid\nweek period, only sagging a bit southwestward near the AZ-NM\nborder with 500 mb heights remaining in a 596-598 decameter range.\nTemperatures will remain hot and above normal with backdoor\nfrontal intrusions only offering a couple to a few degrees of\ncooling in northeastern to east central zones Tuesday and more-so\non Wednesday. With the high positioning slightly southwestward,\nthis will introduce more northwesterly flow aloft over the\nnortheastern NM zones. This coupled with northerly or easterly\nsurface winds, will raise bulk shear values and increase the\nsevere threat while higher dewpoints, upslope, and surface\nconvergence also contribute. Storm coverage may undergo a slight\nreduction over the southwestern mountains and Continental Divide \nTuesday and Wednesday, but should regain potential into Thursday\nas the moist backdoor frontal air mass gets convectively aided and\nshoved farther west. \n\nInto Friday, the high would remain close to the AZ-NM border with\nstorms being reliant on diurnal heating and recycling of \nThursday's moisture. By next weekend, the upper high looks to \nweaken and begin elongating west-to-east with drier air pivoting \ninto NM from the northwest. The GFS and GEFS ensemble mean hints \nat another shortwave dropping into northeastern NM which would \ngenerate additional storms, but European and Canadian solutions do\nnot appear as favorable. Consequently, the forecast for next \nweekend leans on the drier side with temperatures running a few to\nseveral degrees above normal for all areas.",
